<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#papiamento">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Papiamento</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Papiamentu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Papiamento is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> language spoken on Aruba, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonaire">Bonaire</a> and Curaçao in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. Its vocabulary draws mainly on Portuguese and Spanish, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English and African influences, and it developed among enslaved Africans and settlers from the 17th century. It is an official language of Aruba and Curaçao alongside Dutch.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/papiamento.jpg" alt="Papiamento" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CROIX · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_di_papiamentu.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
